			<div class="content" id="post-content-4273414">
									

<p>Hello, Italian user here, I really never write on the site but I read (and lurk) a lot.<br />
This time as I thin it could be useful, I need to make some statement about the use of my native language here. Just excuse me if I'm wordy, I'm just not very used to write in English.</p>
<p>[[html]]<br />
Cpt. DeLuca reaches the bottom of the stairs, and stands across from SCP-4621-2, in a defensive stance, detailed as &quot;Tutta porta di ferro&quot; (full iron door).<br />
[[/html]]</p>
<p>&quot;Tutta porta di ferro&quot; is almost nosense in Italian. As I understand you tried to backward translate &quot;full iron door&quot; back in italian, but we word that kind of thing a bit different. I would suggest &quot;Portone di ferro&quot; as a more friendly and meaning-wise translation of &quot;full iron door&quot;. Also a more literal one could be &quot;Portone di ferro massiccio&quot; (massiccio for the &quot;full&quot; part).</p>
<p>Anyways if we want to conceive an appropriate name for a defensive stance in Italian, &quot;Muro di Ferro&quot; or &quot;Iron Wall&quot; in english would bettere fit it. The wall is better suited to convey the meaning of something that should be defensive in Italian, although a big door (&quot;Portone&quot;) could also easily fit the role.</p>
<p>Translation aside, really a nice work of narrative here and a very nice story-behind-the-scp. A well deserved +1.</p>

							</div>

								
					
